{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"Testicular cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of one or both testicles.","source_span":"Testicular cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of one or both testicles."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"Testicular cancer is the most common cancer in men aged 15 to 34 years.","source_span":"Testicular cancer is the most common cancer in men aged 15 to 34 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"Testicular cancer can usually be cured, even in late stages of the disease.","source_span":"Testicular cancer can usually be cured, even in late stages of the disease."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"Cryptorchidism (an undescended testicle) is a risk factor for testicular cancer.","source_span":"A condition called cryptorchidism (an undescended testicle) is a risk factor for testicular cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"White men are four times more likely than black men to have testicular cancer.","source_span":"White men are four times more likely than black men to have testicular cancer"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"The number of new cases of testicular cancer has doubled in the last 40 years, but deaths have decreased greatly due to better treatments.","source_span":"the number of new cases of testicular cancer has doubled in the last 40 years, the number of deaths caused by testicular cancer has decreased greatly because of better treatments"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"The two main types of testicular germ cell tumors are seminomas and nonseminomas.","source_span":"The two main types of testicular germ cell tumors are seminomas and nonseminomas."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"Klinefelter syndrome is a risk factor for testicular cancer.","source_span":"Having Klinefelter syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"The testicles are the male sex glands and make testosterone and sperm.","source_span":"The testicles are the male sex glands and make testosterone and sperm."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","statement":"Having a personal or family history of testicular cancer is a risk factor for testicular cancer.","source_span":"Having a personal or family history of testicular cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which age group has the highest rate of testicular cancer?","answer":"Testicular cancer is the most common cancer in men aged 15 to 34 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How much more likely are white men to develop testicular cancer compared to black men?","answer":"White men are four times more likely than black men to have testicular cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the two main types of testicular germ cell tumors?","answer":"The two main types of testicular germ cell tumors are seminomas and nonseminomas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What condition involving an undescended testicle increases the risk of testicular cancer?","answer":"Cryptorchidism, a condition where a testicle has not descended, is a risk factor for testicular cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What two things do the testicles produce?","answer":"The testicles are the male sex glands and make testosterone and sperm."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Even though new cases of testicular cancer have doubled in the last 40 years, what has happened to the death rate?","answer":"The death rate has decreased greatly during that time because of better treatments."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"Gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) is a group of rare diseases in which abnormal trophoblast cells grow inside the uterus after conception.","source_span":"Gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) is a group of rare diseases in which abnormal trophoblast cells grow inside the uterus after conception."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"Hydatidiform mole (HM) is the most common type of GTD.","source_span":"Hydatidiform mole (HM) is the most common type of GTD."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) is a type of GTD that is almost always malignant.","source_span":"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) is a type of gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) that is almost always malignant."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"A complete hydatidiform mole forms when sperm fertilizes an egg that does not contain the mother's DNA.","source_span":"A complete HM forms when sperm fertilizes an egg that does not contain the mothers DNA."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"A partial hydatidiform mole forms when sperm fertilizes a normal egg and there are two sets of DNA from the father in the fertilized egg.","source_span":"A partial HM forms when sperm fertilizes a normal egg and there are two sets of DNA from the father in the fertilized egg."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"An ovarian cyst larger than 6 centimeters is a risk factor that increases the chance a hydatidiform mole will become cancer.","source_span":"An ovarian cyst larger than 6 centimeters."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"A pregnancy before age 20 or after age 35 is a risk factor for GTD.","source_span":"Being pregnant when you are younger than 20 or older than 35 years of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"Choriocarcinoma is more likely to form in women who have had a molar pregnancy, especially with a complete hydatidiform mole, a normal pregnancy, a tubal pregnancy, or a miscarriage.","source_span":"A choriocarcinoma is more likely to form in women who have had any of the following: Molar pregnancy, especially with a complete hydatidiform mole. Normal pregnancy. Tubal pregnancy... Miscarriage."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"A placental-site trophoblastic tumor (PSTT) grows very slowly, and signs or symptoms may appear months or years after a normal pregnancy.","source_span":"A PSTT grows very slowly and signs or symptoms may appear months or years after a normal pregnancy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","statement":"Signs of GTD include abnormal vaginal bleeding and a uterus that is larger than normal.","source_span":"Signs of GTD include abnormal vaginal bleeding and a uterus that is larger than normal."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is the most common type of gestational trophoblastic disease?","answer":"Hydatidiform mole (HM) is the most common type of gestational trophoblastic disease."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"Genetically, how does a complete hydatidiform mole differ from a partial hydatidiform mole?","answer":"A complete mole forms when sperm fertilizes an egg lacking the mother's DNA, so the tissue has only the father's DNA. A partial mole forms when sperm fertilizes a normal egg but contributes two sets of paternal DNA."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What ovarian cyst size is considered a risk factor for a hydatidiform mole turning into cancer?","answer":"An ovarian cyst larger than 6 centimeters is a risk factor for a hydatidiform mole becoming cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"At what maternal ages is the risk of gestational trophoblastic disease increased?","answer":"Being pregnant younger than 20 or older than 35 years of age increases the risk of GTD."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What pregnancy history is most associated with a higher likelihood of developing choriocarcinoma?","answer":"Choriocarcinoma is more likely in women who have had a molar pregnancy (especially a complete hydatidiform mole), a normal pregnancy, a tubal pregnancy, or a miscarriage."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000022_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What are typical signs of gestational trophoblastic disease?","answer":"Signs of GTD include abnormal vaginal bleeding and a uterus that is larger than normal."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH) is a rare cancer that begins in LCH cells, a type of dendritic cell which fights infection.","source_span":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH) is a rare cancer that begins in LCH cells (a type of dendritic cell which fights infection)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"LCH may occur at any age but is most common in young children.","source_span":"LCH may occur at any age, but is most common in young children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Mutations of the BRAF gene are among the mutations that occur in LCH cells and may make them grow and multiply quickly.","source_span":"These include mutations of the BRAF gene. These changes may make the LCH cells grow and multiply quickly."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Having a parent who was exposed to certain chemicals such as benzene is a risk factor for LCH.","source_span":"Having a parent who was exposed to certain chemicals such as benzene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Having a parent who was exposed to metal, granite, or wood dust in the workplace is a risk factor for LCH.","source_span":"Having a parent who was exposed to metal, granite, or wood dust in the workplace."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Being Hispanic is listed as a risk factor for LCH.","source_span":"Being Hispanic."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Children with LCH lesions in bones around the ears or eyes have a high risk for diabetes insipidus and other central nervous system disease.","source_span":"Children with LCH lesions in bones around the ears or eyes have a high risk for diabetes insipidus and other central nervous system disease."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Diabetes insipidus, a symptom of LCH affecting the pituitary gland, can cause a strong thirst and frequent urination.","source_span":"Diabetes insipidus. This can cause a strong thirst and frequent urination."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","statement":"Superior vena cava syndrome from LCH affecting the lymph nodes or thymus can cause coughing, trouble breathing, and swelling of the face, neck, and upper arms.","source_span":"Superior vena cava syndrome. This can cause coughing, trouble breathing, and swelling of the face, neck, and upper arms."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What type of cell does Langerhans cell histiocytosis begin in?","answer":"LCH begins in LCH cells, a type of dendritic cell that normally fights infection."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which gene mutation is associated with abnormal growth of LCH cells?","answer":"Mutations of the BRAF gene are among the changes seen in LCH cells that may make them grow and multiply quickly."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What occupational or chemical exposures in a parent are listed as risk factors for a child developing LCH?","answer":"Risk factors include having a parent exposed to chemicals such as benzene, or to metal, granite, or wood dust in the workplace."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What condition are children at high risk for when LCH lesions occur in bones around the ears or eyes?","answer":"They have a high risk for diabetes insipidus and other central nervous system disease."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What symptoms does diabetes insipidus cause in patients with LCH affecting the pituitary gland?","answer":"It can cause a strong thirst and frequent urination."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000023_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which age group is LCH most commonly seen in?","answer":"LCH can occur at any age but is most common in young children."}
